# Use UniTask, not coroutines

`com.cysharp.unitask` is a project dependency. **Default to UniTask for every
asynchronous flow.** Do not write new `IEnumerator` / `StartCoroutine` code.

`Assets/Scripts/World/Gate.cs` is the reference implementation — read it before
writing a new async sequence.

## The pattern

Launch from a synchronous entry point with `.Forget()`, pass
`destroyCancellationToken` so the flow dies with the GameObject, and thread that
token through every `await`:

```csharp
private void OpenGate()
{
    OpenGateSequence(destroyCancellationToken).Forget();
}

private async UniTask OpenGateSequence(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    await UniTask.WaitUntil(AllChargesFinishedMovement, cancellationToken: cancellationToken);
    await UniTask.WhenAll(moveTasks);
}
```

## Translations

| Coroutine | UniTask |
|---|---|
| `yield return null` | `await UniTask.Yield(PlayerLoopTiming.Update, cancellationToken)` |
| `yield return new WaitForSeconds(t)` | `await UniTask.WaitForSeconds(t, cancellationToken: token)` |
| `while (!cond) yield return null;` | `await UniTask.WaitUntil(() => cond, cancellationToken: token)` |
| running several coroutines and polling them | `await UniTask.WhenAll(a, b)` |
| `StartCoroutine(X())` | `X(destroyCancellationToken).Forget()` |
| `StopCoroutine` / `StopAllCoroutines` | cancel a `CancellationTokenSource` |

Return `UniTask`, never `async void` — the exceptions are Unity/UI event handlers
and NaughtyAttributes `[Button]` methods, which cannot be awaited.

## When a coroutine is still acceptable

Only these three cases, and say which one applies in a comment:

1. An external API hands you an `IEnumerator` and accepts nothing else.
2. Editor-only code that must run outside play mode.
3. A small edit inside an already coroutine-based file where converting the whole
   file is out of scope. Match the file — never mix both styles in one type.
